pcm: skip the address generator's dead fourth step

The voice loop walks the wave address forward five times per slot and reads the
wave ROM at each stop, but the last stop is only ever used when the voice is
pitched at four or more wave samples per output sample. sub_phase_of is
(sub_phase >> 14) & 7, where sub_phase is a 14-bit accumulator plus a 16-bit
increment, so it is 0..4; everything after the third increment feeds only the
`sub_phase_of >= 3` and `>= 4` updates and the samp3 term of the DPCM
accumulation, which is itself guarded on `>= 4`. b15 is deliberately not
advanced past step 3 in the original, and address_cmp, address_cnt, cmp1, cmp2
and nibble_cmp6 are all dead afterwards, so below the threshold the whole tail
-- including one of the five wave ROM reads -- can be skipped outright.

Because the win is data-dependent the harness grew two modes with the phase
increments spread across the full 16-bit range, so sub_phase_of covers 0..4
rather than the 0..1 that ordinary pitches produce. Both are in the numbers
below.

Measured on the ROM-free voice-loop harness, all 32 voices keyed on:
29577 -> 27741 Ir per tick (-6.2%) at ordinary pitches, and 30482 -> 28899
(-5.2%) with sub_phase_of spread over 0..4 including 24% of slots at 4, where
the step cannot be skipped at all.

